  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Daily severe weather threat through the end of \nthe week.\n\nThe original Mesoscale Convective Complex that moved out of\nPennsylvania into our CWA late this morning has now bowed well \nsouth and east out over the Delmarva and central Chesapeake \nBay and is aligned to the west and northwest into northeastern\nVirginia and northwest into eastern West Virginia. Winds have\ngusted between 45 to 65 mph within this complex, while isolated\nsupercell thunderstorms that have become severe from Hampshire\nCounty to Fauquier County are producing large hail with damaging\nwinds. Round two of thunderstorm clusters could develop\nsomewhere over western or northwest Pennsylvania later this\nafternoon before racing east and southeast again toward our\narea. Confidence in timing and initiation of such thunderstorms\nare uncertain. We almost need to wait until storms start\ndeveloping then we can track it and time better. This particular\nsetup of transferring from Round 1 to Round 2 may be similar\nshould be get Round 3 or 4 overnight tonight through Thursday\nnight. By Friday, cold front should clear much of the forecast \narea causing a stabilizing effect limiting the risk of severe \nweather, but showers and thunderstorms will still be possible \nnorth of the sfc front. Timing seems to slow with later model\nruns of this outcome. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot and humid conditions yield likely record \nhigh minimum temperatures this week.\n\nDecent model agreement that areas south of US-33 and US-211 will\nsee heat indices meet heat advisory criteria this afternoon.\nBased on yesterday's high temperature readings and greater\nconfidence, issued Heat Advisories for the southern half of the\nfcst area. \n\nThursday's max temperature forecast has a lot more \nuncertainty due to potential for more convection. If convection\ndoesn't materialize, more record high minimums are possible\nagain today, but that doesn't seem likely at this time, except\nonly at Charlottesville. \n\nRecord high minimum temperatures were set yesterday at IAD, DCA,\nand HGR. RERs were issued earlier. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Heat & thunderstorms begin to decrease heading into \nLabor Day weekend.\n\nHigh pressure tracks southeast from the Great Lakes on Saturday, \npushing a cold front through region throughout the day. Some \nuncertainty still exists regarding how far south this front \nwill advance south before stalling, but most ensemble members \ndepict this boundary south of our region by Saturday afternoon. \nThis should bring a drop in heat & humidity across the Mid-\nAtlantic, with afternoon highs ranging in the mid-80s on \nSaturday. As mid-level dry air continues to track across the \narea, temperatures should continue to decrease as the weekened \ngoes on. By the Labor Day holiday, it should feel relatively \nmild & dry, with dewpoints ranging in the upper-50s across the \nregion.",
